#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161852 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162052 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233ee0d9-ce64-5d75-ac81-351ec0ac8f4b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transformations Where the Change-of-Variables Method Is Applicable"}] :question/id #uuid "14830c55-32bd-5eb9-adb6-b91675061a21" :question/math-academy-id "e-9100" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y = X^4,$ where $X\\in (0,2)$\n\nII. $Y = \\cos X,$ where $X\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$\n\nIII. $Y = e^{-X},$ where $X\\in (0, \\infty)$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y = u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x) = x^4$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,2).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. For example, $u(x) = \\cos x$ is decreasing on $x \\in \\left(0,\\pi \\right)$ and increasing on $x \\in \\left(\\pi,2\\pi\\right).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x) =e^{-x}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161853 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26854b8e-68bc-5e69-8fcd-fa46b72d05c3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "2fd76ea7-ab08-5afa-9762-158a85999321" :question/math-academy-id "e-9101" :question/problem "Let ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) be a continuous random variable with the following probability