variables method, for nonzero ![](images/49/4958bae6d475dfd7b69ba62c4206ff577a7aca89978d096a7255df5de68dc7d4.svg) we have\n\n![](images/33/33abe4f49bf243c393397ab38ea454192378fd9c16868e6c9e1dd699625c8ebd.svg)\n\nFinally, since ![](images/e5/e548073e412ef1d2ce28be65200ecee6d2de2d313f96926efc3f502c882a8b63.svg) is nonzero on ![](images/60/60ded9db077de95e8e12a1697b6e1cfcb668709e61711a2de9235f93b1e43e47.svg) the bounds are ![](images/0d/0de2ecb189258e452bbbd4c453016da8d6a7ec1857d973f1591bc9ca5acc02f3.svg) and ![](images/24/24db93fbc4799e38a2ce26b39cf98b38c6ab418b087a71fe3aab35a0d5f525a6.svg) we have\n![](images/96/96c9789a1c16d9f1c042fc312c26cd96cfb1942c88bc282092c8e92baa60d981.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability density function of ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) is\n\n![](images/07/07515c45f97b814ea1a7a01ca8b9ecf43a7f2e54c0a203d86653ccda3110f309.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161854 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "fd8ceb2f-55e5-588f-ae79-015f58007fba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9102" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/b4/b48edaa6312ced870bff2220f801f4e2da867a6665db2126311f2cfe28a4d558.svg)\n\nIf $Y = \\sqrt{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X \\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n![](images/ca/cacd5fa85f055d885fabb6ffa2cf62788d88482eeaef327182d052f6767b3a3b.svg)\n\nHere, we have $Y = \\sqrt{X}.$ The transformation $u(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find $u^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $(u^{-1})'(y){:}$\n\n$$\n(u^{-1})'(y) = 2y\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to the change-of-variable method, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({y}^{2})\\cdot |2y| \\\\  & =3({y}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =3{y}^{4}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =6{y}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $f_X$ is nonzero on $0 < x < 1,$ the bounds are $a = 0$ and $b = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0) = 0, \\quad u(1) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n![](images/e2/e20ebb32777cc624f0c436e59cb0be55958ab8369b345a7da890a3504b42fe34.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b6e1b5-4780-54aa-8a89-2c389618ccea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}, & 1<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555047} {:answer/id #uuid "60d2bd5c-2906-5c77-9a51-8fcb2a54de03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{y}, & e<y<{e}^{4} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555216} {:answer/id #uuid "9979ab92-aa46-551c-9b7a-d8c01b8826ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{28}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y, & 0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555371} {:answer/id #uuid "aaaffa65-e7fc-5b99-b05e-ff602e8d8690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{28}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y, & 1<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555408} {:answer/id #uuid "d267d30c-5457-580f-9c78-b2e138759bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}, & 0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d267d30c-5457-580f-9c78-b2e138759bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}, & 0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555498} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a88399b-1bc1-50e9-b55f-1a6bb18a1b16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fc46307-e5a1-551b-b8cf-8fa5407926f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109183" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}, & 1<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)={e}^{y}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({e}^{y})\\cdot |{e}^{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{{e}^{y}}\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{y/2}\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<4,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=0,\\,u(4)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}, & 0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162052 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233ee0d9-ce64-5d75-ac81-351ec0ac8f4b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transformations Where the Change-of-Variables Method Is Applicable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cff4110-673e-5a0b-b763-dc2b18f6751a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555024} {:answer/id #uuid "97d11451-774d-5db5-b500-02196e6f3a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555362} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4db205-357c-52d8-b50a-8c12abf3a01b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555448} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ff1c98-2b1a-51f1-8894-d27bfa81a014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555523} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d292c9-efca-5a44-b466-139650cf2579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8ff1c98-2b1a-51f1-8894-d27bfa81a014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555523} :answer-field/id #uuid "1654b677-b5f3-51ca-bcd6-9d948b5c36fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83ef6537-86b1-5578-a678-ff969ac2e505" :question/math-academy-id "q-143755" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=-3X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ where $X\\in (1,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $Y=\\sqrt{X},$ where $X\\in (0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-3x$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162052 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233ee0d9-ce64-5d75-ac81-351ec0ac8f4b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transformations Where the Change-of-Variables Method Is Applicable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "522a23da-3199-5beb-840f-bbd80050fea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555185} {:answer/id #uuid "7a26b778-f0af-5c3d-bf08-abac5119d76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555284} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e6decc-fb57-5713-ab4c-0492b3b343b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555512} {:answer/id #uuid "e8447dd5-f351-54b0-b319-349538194370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555570} {:answer/id #uuid "f426aa49-b205-5b56-8c2b-7dfee92d75ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a26b778-f0af-5c3d-bf08-abac5119d76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555284} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3f18f8-3eb5-5f43-835e-c32778388a91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6df473a-21d5-53f9-84c7-d9c14b1c57fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-143756" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ where $X\\in [1,\\infty )$\n\nII. $Y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡X,$ where $X\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{3},$ where $X\\in (-10,10)$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in [1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{3}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-10,10).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162052 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233ee0d9-ce64-5d75-ac81-351ec0ac8f4b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transformations Where the Change-of-Variables Method Is Applicable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbec0d8-f92e-5399-9366-340075456307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555030} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc62f23-318c-50d0-87d4-4f837695b5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555054} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9bd6b5-dd49-56b3-aad5-18d9bbb43d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555058} {:answer/id #uuid "c158ba71-83f0-55e5-ab39-f9b125bbc5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555466} {:answer/id #uuid "df26bafd-3008-5692-9ec4-c15e5e10d340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f9bd6b5-dd49-56b3-aad5-18d9bbb43d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555058} :answer-field/id #uuid "2306df6b-e9b2-5ebc-be81-284b6041ab6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68ad658e-fc68-5dd0-8313-20d02f0dbcab" :question/math-academy-id "q-143757" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y={X}^{5}+1,$ where $X\\in (-1,1)$\n\nII. $Y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2X,$ where $X\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $Y=1-{X}^{4},$ where $X\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{5}+1$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Transformation III is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=1-{x}^{4}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26854b8e-68bc-5e69-8fcd-fa46b72d05c3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4010751e-b00b-5952-82c6-80435f82ec17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555139} {:answer/id #uuid "936d9658-283d-560e-bc6d-ea190c5bec8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{(y+4)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555349} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9ede15-0d30-5234-a411-d8e0859ef10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555418} {:answer/id #uuid "bad0b915-0433-5599-97e8-4708519f4503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(y+4)^{2}}, & -1<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555444} {:answer/id #uuid "d6231f98-9851-5b39-adff-92d72af76228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6231f98-9851-5b39-adff-92d72af76228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555509} :answer-field/id #uuid "0feccc34-587e-5131-85c6-09d92716448a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d414a04e-d0c3-5f7e-b95a-0d762c3601e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143764" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 2<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X-2,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X-2.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x-2$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}y-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1}(y)-2 \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(2y+4)\\cdot |2| \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2y+4)^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $2<x<4,$ the bounds are $a=2$ and $b=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(2)=-1,\\,u(4)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162052 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233ee0d9-ce64-5d75-ac81-351ec0ac8f4b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transformations Where the Change-of-Variables Method Is Applicable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12750671-c0a4-56d1-8642-65229985be21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555036} {:answer/id #uuid "41aa6d50-25e0-5074-be28-acd0423987c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555143} {:answer/id #uuid "4853f5fd-68e8-592c-9f5d-4579766aa230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555156} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a74afd-b188-5b3c-8185-ce3e76a3ea4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555564} {:answer/id #uuid "f44afbbd-61fb-51ca-818f-6b079b7bcfa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4853f5fd-68e8-592c-9f5d-4579766aa230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555156} :answer-field/id #uuid "d94ef671-f7de-5c89-80ee-f193344cab7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b66e4d9-8056-570c-838a-8b4a8b4a119c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143767" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=\\frac{1}{{X}^{4}+1},$ where $X\\in (-2,2)$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{2}+X,$ where $X\\in (0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+1}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-2,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,2).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{2}+x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\n.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26854b8e-68bc-5e69-8fcd-fa46b72d05c3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45a16446-72e7-5554-b9f3-37b2398be8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-3\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<4\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555150} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4b3c40-e6b8-525a-82c8-7ceba383c32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-3\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<4\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555198} {:answer/id #uuid "ef12e410-2dd2-54b0-8f94-d0d3d23c11aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555589} {:answer/id #uuid "f5eb8add-1881-5e5c-861b-7348e0c1f821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555610} {:answer/id #uuid "fe44390e-3a59-58fc-9d2b-ca5ec6e5fa5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5eb8add-1881-5e5c-861b-7348e0c1f821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555610} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92ab0a5-3040-53cf-82b8-bf078d4d8e28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6f869b2-aa11-5377-8e76-cb36213ba7a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143773" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}\\, & 0<x<\\pi  \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=3X+3\\pi ,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=3X+3\\pi .$ The transformation $u(x)=3x+3\\pi $ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =3y+3\\pi  \\\\ y & =3{u}^{-1}(y)+3\\pi  \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi )\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<\\pi ,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=3\\pi ,\\,u(\\pi )=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26854b8e-68bc-5e69-8fcd-fa46b72d05c3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555029} {:answer/id #uuid "10021868-bebf-5d55-88d6-1666034c1a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555031} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef13b49-b8a1-573d-960c-ebf7e8cd471e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555056} {:answer/id #uuid "df0e68c0-8aa4-5c57-a44f-b03b70691336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555543} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bcd3f9-a718-5576-984c-731f9180bd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y/2}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555029} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ea4d1f3-3230-5c90-8f5f-f708753efce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10780b62-18db-53fc-94bd-63d320bb4c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-143774" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2y \\\\ y & =2{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & ={e}^{1-y/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26854b8e-68bc-5e69-8fcd-fa46b72d05c3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5505216d-62d4-5def-93fc-88b1ec673ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-y)^{3}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555191} {:answer/id #uuid "94632b8d-e646-5a1c-841b-4e1b8e19abb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(1-y)^{3}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555351} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9cd992-4d6d-5ca5-ae3d-ef1729cd1a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(2-y)^{3}}{15}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555407} {:answer/id #uuid "d0da31ad-07c0-5d9c-9e30-027174062da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555496} {:answer/id #uuid "d3decfd7-864b-5543-84c0-099e06bf2958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(2-y)^{3}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3decfd7-864b-5543-84c0-099e06bf2958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(2-y)^{3}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555502} :answer-field/id #uuid "316bf6ab-3947-532a-8211-ad09e1e9e024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba6a48c1-7aec-5ddc-a819-dca70319c91f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143775" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3},\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2-X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2-X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2-x$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2-y \\\\ y & =2-{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-1\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(2-y)\\cdot |-1| \\\\  & =4(2-y)^{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(2-y)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=2,\\,u(1)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(2-y)^{3}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26854b8e-68bc-5e69-8fcd-fa46b72d05c3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172b375e-317c-5b1c-9b41-2c052f27e8cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555044} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8f09eb-0cd1-51e6-8310-03cf1c596658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555163} {:answer/id #uuid "5da7260c-a3fd-55ce-8eb2-dbff9b05485e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{y}, & 1\\le y\\le \\sqrt{e} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555210} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d09561-afb0-5c88-aa32-fb6a6e01b8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2y}, & 1\\le y\\le \\sqrt{e} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555476} {:answer/id #uuid "d70b1e5b-84cc-5206-9db3-4b373b7356ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d70b1e5b-84cc-5206-9db3-4b373b7356ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555514} :answer-field/id #uuid "7865a740-1a99-5f05-a821-db2d459c2cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cdff0a1-3127-5db2-a31d-94794e2a46b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143777" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}\\, & 1\\le x\\le e \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{X}{2},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{X}{2}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{{u}^{-1}(y)}{2} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2y}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<e,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=e,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,u(e)=\\frac{e}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162052 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233ee0d9-ce64-5d75-ac81-351ec0ac8f4b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transformations Where the Change-of-Variables Method Is Applicable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2beb3def-714b-5fac-9aed-08a84b6a5e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555097} {:answer/id #uuid "51140ee9-ed10-5c84-b02a-584e6f2c21e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555180} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed4ff03-924d-5bea-be95-9eab56a5cd78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555333} {:answer/id #uuid "931f53f1-45e2-561b-b5a4-0a96990a5a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555345} {:answer/id #uuid "fe47ec8f-29bf-5b7d-8c7e-65bb3449320f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ed4ff03-924d-5bea-be95-9eab56a5cd78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555333} :answer-field/id #uuid "4afdfcbd-f7ba-5636-a6c2-9bd26c6f3ed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2be8e1ac-e4a4-5fc5-ab2e-be1784629b6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143779" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=-X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=-{X}^{2},$ where $X\\in (-1,1)$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{4},$ where $X\\in (-\\infty ,0)$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-x$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-1,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,1).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{4}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83866d01-d00c-5606-97fc-8034676dfc07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555303} {:answer/id #uuid "af72d3b7-3cf4-586f-afbf-29d7d4a06a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555420} {:answer/id #uuid "bebaa45c-6d1d-5ae9-aa82-d69d094bf6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2y, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555455} {:answer/id #uuid "e32bb70d-69d8-5b13-87d0-5ff4152c7216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{y}^{2}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555555} {:answer/id #uuid "fbaccb16-ff2d-5f45-9895-60c746ff8274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{y}^{3}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbaccb16-ff2d-5f45-9895-60c746ff8274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{y}^{3}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555624} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa1eb7e-f2a5-549e-b007-219daa3b78ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "674cd7aa-b875-5b4c-b5f8-f279a4fd931b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143892" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=-\\sqrt{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=-\\sqrt{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=-\\sqrt{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2y\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =2({y}^{2})\\cdot 2|y| \\\\  & =4{y}^{2}|y|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(1)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{y}^{3}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2de2ebeb-e356-5986-9a18-4bbd04698ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555103} {:answer/id #uuid "4410421e-5953-53d0-9979-891add3b5dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{y}^{9}}{512}, & 0<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555145} {:answer/id #uuid "4844bff0-8c04-50b9-8d99-cfda0d95e6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2}, & 0<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555155} {:answer/id #uuid "c0334f0c-f3c9-5d23-a132-9151568d6904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{2}}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555461} {:answer/id #uuid "d7241d2b-cc21-532e-9835-5918240ff0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{5}}{2}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2de2ebeb-e356-5986-9a18-4bbd04698ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555103} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfdb681-1420-5e1f-89b1-59fd61ccc9df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff19e38b-ccbe-5836-a668-3a89307b9227" :question/math-academy-id "q-143894" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}, & 0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\sqrt[3]{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\sqrt[3]{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({y}^{3})\\cdot |3{y}^{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{({y}^{3})}^{3}}{4}\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{9}}{4}\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<2,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(2)=\\sqrt[3]{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19341623-c069-57f0-918f-2be57be5a81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555046} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb89bec-79dd-58af-8196-d3d356437ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\sqrt{y}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555135} {:answer/id #uuid "533a4289-18f4-5f2c-abf2-84425ff4dc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{y}}{2}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555188} {:answer/id #uuid "c386f9b3-75b7-5c3c-8088-0bdc0612b3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555473} {:answer/id #uuid "f11f15bf-525a-5a41-bdc7-38a742a7f4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c386f9b3-75b7-5c3c-8088-0bdc0612b3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555473} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f86259-5958-5bf3-bfcc-15c37733af30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ba326fb-1af3-5ddd-8698-9863f73e0845" :question/math-academy-id "q-143895" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={X}^{2},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y={X}^{2}.$ The transformation $u(x)={x}^{2}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ y & =({u}^{-1}(y))^{2} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =(2-2\\sqrt{y})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(1)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246d1671-2920-5107-938a-d5818924161b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{2}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555072} {:answer/id #uuid "408fabb1-72b8-5f80-b166-4b2097274a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y, & 1<y<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555141} {:answer/id #uuid "643a424e-3a5b-5283-bde6-ee03fbed2dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555226} {:answer/id #uuid "858132b6-0402-5b5e-b535-8e91c02ecb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{y-2}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555307} {:answer/id #uuid "94dcdc7d-dc32-5d1c-b053-4b6089835648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{e-1}, & 1<y<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "643a424e-3a5b-5283-bde6-ee03fbed2dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555226} :answer-field/id #uuid "02e0b4df-b03a-5331-8fad-a3680e2a7c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f7b4c3d-9f53-5a0a-9f65-57f007102f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-143899" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}, & 1<x<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y})\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{y})}{(\\frac{1}{y})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<e,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=e,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=1,\\,u(e)={e}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
